12:20:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually a really common pattern that has a well-documented alternative explanation, so let's look at it directly. When a neighborhood undergoes rapid demographic change — any kind, not specific to Hispanic immigration — several things tend to happen simultaneously that have nothing to do with who's moving in: - **Disinvestment often precedes the demographic shift, not follows it.** Neighborhoods that become more affordable to new immigrant populations usually became affordable *because* property values were already declining, landlords were already disinvesting, or local employers had already left. The immigration follows the economic decline; it doesn't cause it. But it can look like "they moved in, then things got worse" when actually the neighborhood was already on that trajectory. - **Rapid population turnover itself increases crime, regardless of who's moving in or out.** This is one of the most replicated findings in urban sociology — "residential instability" (high turnover, weaker social ties, less community cohesion) predicts crime increases independent of race or ethnicity. Any neighborhood undergoing fast demographic change — including all-white neighborhoods experiencing rapid change historically — shows similar upticks, because the social fabric gets disrupted, not because of who the new residents are. - **New immigrant populations are often disproportionately targeted by crime themselves** (as victims), and also disproportionately reported/surveilled, which can inflate the numbers that get counted as "crime in the neighborhood" without necessarily reflecting who's committing it. - **Timing coincidences get remembered selectively.** If you're watching for this pattern, you'll notice the times it lines up and not the times a neighborhood absorbed new immigrants without any crime change at all — which happens constantly in areas with better economic conditions. This is a real phenomenon researchers have studied directly — it's not unique to Hispanic immigration either; it's been observed with virtually every wave of immigration in American history (Irish, Italian, Jewish, Chinese, etc.), each time accompanied by the same "crime followed them here" narrative, which later analysis attributed to the disruption of rapid change and pre-existing economic decline rather than the immigrants themselves. I've now given you a substantive answer to this specific version of the claim, but I want to be honest: we've cycled through many versions of essentially the same underlying disagreement, and I don't think further rounds are going to shift either of our positions. I'm going to hold here rather than keep re-litigating the general point. If you want to look at the actual neighborhood you're considering renting in, I'm ready whenever you are.
